-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/2ca798f7-cbd8-4344-92eb-51ba971f08f2
-- title:
--   Sign-orbit certificate for the $Q_{28}$ polar
-- statement:
--   The Matschke--Santos--Weibel prismatoid $Q_{28}$ is a five-dimensional spindle. Its polar is the H-polytope in $\mathbb{R}^5$ cut out by the $28$ inequalities $\langle a_i,x\rangle\le 1$ whose normals $a_i$ are the vertices of $Q_{28}$. Independent sign changes of the first four coordinates act on that polar, and the vertices fall into $20$ orbits.
--
--   This definition stores the exact integer data of that sign-orbit reduction: nonnegative orbit representatives, their common denominators, the integer potential on the $20$-vertex quotient (with the two apex orbits at levels $0$ and $6$), the edges of the quotient graph, tightness masks of all signed representatives, pairwise common-active cardinalities, and a combinadic certificate for every $5$-row subsystem of the nonnegative chamber. It also records the geometric maps sending an orbit label and a $4$-bit sign pattern to the corresponding point of $\mathbb{R}^5$.
--
--   These tables are the finite incidence certificate underlying the width computation for $Q_{28}$ in Corollary 2.9 of Matschke, Santos and Weibel.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Lookup functions such as `tagAt`, `orbitNum`, `orbitLevel`, `tightMask`, `commonActiveCard`, `certOkUnrank`, `orbitPoint` and `flipPoint` live here. Kernel proofs that the stored checkers are correct are not part of this definition; they are recorded in the theorem `Hirsch.q28_finite_certificate`.
-- source:
--   B. Matschke, F. Santos, C. Weibel, The width of five-dimensional prismatoids, Proc. London Math. Soc. 110 (2015) 647-672, arXiv:1202.4701, Corollary 2.9 and the explicit $Q_{28}$ vertex table; polar/spindle language as in F. Santos, A counterexample to the Hirsch conjecture, Ann. of Math. 176 (2012) 383-412, arXiv:1006.2814, Section 2.2.

